# Depictio Docker Compose Configuration
#
# A .env file is OPTIONAL. The single-user quick start (`docker compose up -d`)
# works with no .env at all. Copy this file to .env only when you need a multi-
# user deployment or want to override the local defaults.
# Full reference: https://depictio.github.io/depictio-docs/stable/installation/env-reference/

# ── Application version ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave unset to use the stable release pinned in docker-compose.yaml. Override
# only to run a different build, e.g. `next` (bleeding edge off main) or a
# specific tag like 1.0.0.
# DEPICTIO_VERSION=next

# ── Auth mode ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default (unset) is single-user mode: no login, no secret enforcement — local
# use only. Set to false for a multi-user deployment with login; the server then
# REFUSES to boot until the MinIO and admin passwords below are strong.
# DEPICTIO_AUTH_SINGLE_USER_MODE=false

# ── MinIO storage credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The root single-user quick start IGNORES these — docker-compose.yaml already
# defaults to minio/minio123. They apply when you copy this file to .env for a
# multi-user deployment, the dev compose, or CI (docker-compose.dev.yaml requires
# them). Multi-user mode rejects weak passwords (≥8 chars) — generate a strong
# one:  openssl rand -base64 24
DEPICTIO_MINIO_ROOT_USER=depictio
DEPICTIO_MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=CHANGEME_GENERATE_WITH_openssl_rand_base64_24

# ── First-boot admin ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Seeds the first admin on a fresh database. Not needed for the single-user quick
# start (it auto-seeds a local admin). Required for multi-user mode; set on first
# start — subsequent starts are no-ops, so UI password changes survive restarts
# and PVC wipes. Password ≥8 chars, not a well-known default (changeme, etc.).
DEPICTIO_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
DEPICTIO_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGEME_GENERATE_WITH_openssl_rand_base64_24

# ── Optional CI / Cypress fixture user ────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave seed_test_user=false (the default) in any public-facing deployment.
# CI / e2e environments set it to true so the existing test fixtures keep
# resolving test_user@example.com / test_pwd.
# DEPICTIO_BOOTSTRAP_SEED_TEST_USER=true
# DEPICTIO_BOOTSTRAP_TEST_USER_EMAIL=test_user@example.com
# DEPICTIO_BOOTSTRAP_TEST_USER_PASSWORD=test_pwd

# ── Anonymous installation telemetry ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Depictio sends an anonymous, aggregate heartbeat once a day so the project can
# tell how many installations exist and which features are used. Container images
# and the Helm chart are hosted on GHCR, which publishes no pull counts, so this
# is the only signal the maintainers have.
#
# What is sent: a random installation ID, the Depictio version, how it was
# deployed, OS/arch/Python version, which optional features are switched on, and
# deployment size as coarse buckets ("11-50 users", never exact counts).
# What is never sent: emails, names, hostnames, URLs, IP addresses, file paths,
# S3 endpoints, tokens, or anything a user typed.
#
# Inspect the exact payload on your own deployment:
#   GET /depictio/api/v1/utils/telemetry/preview   (admin token required)
# Full description: docs/telemetry.md
#
# To disable, uncomment either of these (DO_NOT_TRACK is the cross-tool standard):
# DEPICTIO_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false
# DO_NOT_TRACK=1
#
# Send only the install identity and version, no size metrics:
# DEPICTIO_TELEMETRY_INCLUDE_USAGE_METRICS=false
#
# Log the payload instead of sending it, to audit it yourself first:
# DEPICTIO_TELEMETRY_DEBUG=true
#
# Telemetry is additionally suppressed automatically in CI, under pytest, and
# when DEPICTIO_MONGODB_WIPE is set.

# ── CORS allowlist for the API ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of allowed origins for cross-origin browser requests.
# Empty (the default) disables credentialed CORS entirely — safe when the
# React viewer is served same-origin via the bundled nginx.
# DEPICTIO_FASTAPI_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com,https://example.com

# ── Remote data sources (URL / manifest scan modes) ───────────────────────────
# A data collection can be bound to a remote https:// or s3:// source instead of
# a local path. Every server-side fetch of such a URL goes through an SSRF
# gateway (depictio/api/v1/remote_fetch.py): hosts resolving to private,
# loopback, link-local or reserved addresses are rejected, each redirect hop is
# re-validated, and downloads are streamed under a size cap and a timeout.
# The backend and the celery worker both read these six variables and must
# agree; with Compose they share this .env, so they do.
#
# Accept plain http:// sources. Off by default; only a local demo server
# needs it. Public sources should stay on https.
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_ALLOW_HTTP=false
#
# Comma-separated host names. WARNING: the allowlist is EXCLUSIVE while set:
# every host not listed is rejected, public ones included, and a listed host
# also bypasses the private-address check (how internal deployments opt in).
# Leave unset to accept any public host that passes the gateway.
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_URL_ALLOWLIST=data.example.org,raw.githubusercontent.com
#
# Comma-separated host names rejected outright. Checked before the allowlist.
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_URL_DENYLIST=denied.example.org
#
# Comma-separated S3 locations readable without credentials, each either
# "bucket" or "bucket/prefix". Empty by default, so unsigned access is opt-in.
# A listed bucket is read with the signature disabled (credentials for someone
# else's open bucket would only be rejected); every other s3:// URL keeps using
# the project or instance credentials. Checked before any request goes out, so
# naming an unlisted bucket never becomes an existence or region oracle.
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_PUBLIC_S3_BUCKETS=ngi-igenomes,nf-core-awsmegatests/ampliseq
#
# Per-download size cap in bytes (default 500 MiB).
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_MAX_DOWNLOAD_BYTES=524288000
#
# Per-request timeout in seconds.
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_TIMEOUT_S=30
#
# Redirect hops followed before giving up; each hop is re-validated.
# DEPICTIO_REMOTE_MAX_REDIRECTS=3

# ── Key material directory ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Holds the JWT signing keypair and the Fernet key that encrypts per-project
# storage credentials (remote S3 sources). The backend and the celery worker
# MUST share it: docker-compose.yaml mounts the `depictio_keys` volume on both
# at the default location, /app/depictio/keys. Override only if you mount the
# keys elsewhere, and then on both services.
# DEPICTIO_AUTH_KEYS_DIR=/app/depictio/keys

# ── Instance branding ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rebrand the deployment: logo, name, a three-color brand palette, chrome
# surfaces, typography and the figure palette. Everything here is optional and
# independent; unset, the app renders in its stock Mantine look.
#
# Admins can edit all of it live from /admin -> Branding without a redeploy —
# those overrides layer on top of what is set here, and a dashboard can in turn
# override any of it for itself (Settings -> Appearance).
#
# Start from a shipped preset: depictio, trec, embl or ocean. Anything set
# below overrides the preset field by field.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_PRESET=trec
#
# Identity. Logo URLs are absolute (https://) or a path this deployment serves.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_APP_NAME=My Core Facility
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_LOGO_URL=https://example.org/assets/facility-logo.svg
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_LOGO_URL_DARK=https://example.org/assets/facility-logo-dark.svg
#
# Brand palette. Hex, or a Mantine palette name (blue, teal, grape, ...). A hex
# color is expanded server-side into a full 10-shade tuple with your color on
# the shade a filled button paints.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_PRIMARY_COLOR=#00a550
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_SECONDARY_COLOR=#1a4f8f
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_TERTIARY_COLOR=#f5a11b
#
# How far the brand reaches: `accent` (default) re-tints the primary accent
# only; `full` also gives the secondary and tertiary the app's existing
# teal/orange accent families, so buttons, tabs, badges and section accents
# all follow the brand.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_TINT_MODE=full
#
# Status colors stay out of the brand reach so pass / warn / fail keep reading
# as meaning. Set them only if your brand has its own.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_SUCCESS_COLOR=#2f9e44
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_WARNING_COLOR=#f08c00
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_DANGER_COLOR=#e03131
#
# Typography and shape. A named font has to be installed or served by this
# deployment — none is fetched for you. Radius is a Mantine token (xs..xl).
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_FONT_FAMILY=Inter, system-ui, sans-serif
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_HEADINGS_FONT_FAMILY=Inter Tight, Inter, sans-serif
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_DEFAULT_RADIUS=sm
#
# Figures. Left unset, the categorical colorway and the continuous scale are
# derived from the brand palette, so there is no second list to keep in step.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_COLORWAY=#00a550,#1a4f8f,#f5a11b,#19e87d,#3c7cc9
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_SEQUENTIAL=#e9f8ef,#b6e8ca,#7fd6a3,#43c07a,#00a550
# Plotly template for figures whose component picks none. Unset means "follow
# the UI color scheme" (Depictio's brand-aware mantine_light / mantine_dark).
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_PLOT_TEMPLATE=simple_white
#
# Escape hatch: a whole brand theme as JSON, for the fields with no flat
# variable of their own (the per-scheme chrome surfaces). Merged UNDER the flat
# variables above.
# DEPICTIO_BRANDING_THEME={"surfaces_light":{"app_bg":"#f8faf9","nav_bg":"#ffffff","heading":"#00514b"}}
